        "byte_range": [
          740263,
          742291
        ],
        "literal_source_text": "\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}=I-\\mathsf{U}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}^{2}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\perp}=-\\mathsf{U}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\,. (B.5)"
      },
      {
        "id": "A4.SS1",
        "coverage": "NODE",
        "reason": "Real complete Hilbert/AE L2, bounded operators, positive implies selfadjoint, unique nonnegative square root. General background uniqueness selected; Markov/chi-square definitions contextual, no dynamics claim.",
        "qualified_raw_slice": {
          "path": "E:/Samplinglib/runs/20261007-companion-priority/pbps-real-defect-root61/next-macro-source62/primary-A4-SS1-dffe0a792ef0.raw.snapshot",
          "raw_bytes": 23471,
          "lf_bytes": 23471,
          "raw_sha256": "22b19e2441e95e4cb6dc49530c7364dc2ff10d0d0b9d8dcfd1347206c98dc8c0",
          "lf_sha256": "22b19e2441e95e4cb6dc49530c7364dc2ff10d0d0b9d8dcfd1347206c98dc8c0"
        },
        "qualified_LF_slice": {
          "path": "E:/Samplinglib/runs/20261007-companion-priority/pbps-real-defect-root61/next-macro-source62/primary-A4-SS1-dffe0a792ef0.LF.snapshot",
          "raw_bytes": 23471,
          "lf_bytes": 23471,
          "raw_sha256": "22b19e2441e95e4cb6dc49530c7364dc2ff10d0d0b9d8dcfd1347206c98dc8c0",
          "lf_sha256": "22b19e2441e95e4cb6dc49530c7364dc2ff10d0d0b9d8dcfd1347206c98dc8c0"
        },
        "byte_range": [
          1348892,
          1372363
        ],
        "literal_source_text": "D.1 Hilbert space and Markov operator conventions Definition D.1 (Hilbert space notation) . Let \\zeta be a probability measure. The real Hilbert space L^{2}(\\zeta) consists of equivalence classes of measurable functions f such that \\int f^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta<\\infty . Its inner product and norm are \\langle f,g\\rangle_{L^{2}(\\zeta)}\\coloneqq\\int fg\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\|f\\|_{L^{2}(\\zeta)}\\coloneqq\\langle f,f\\rangle_{L^{2}(\\zeta)}^{1/2}\\,. (D.1) We write L_{0}^{2}(\\zeta)\\coloneqq\\Bigl\\{f\\in L^{2}(\\zeta):\\int f\\,\\mathrm{d}\\zeta=0\\Bigr\\}\\,. (D.2) When \\zeta=\\pi_{\\eta} , we abbreviate the inner product and norm by \\langle f,g\\rangle and \\|f\\| . Two functions equal \\zeta -almost everywhere represent the same vector. A closed linear subspace \\mathcal{M}\\subseteq L^{2}(\\zeta) has orthogonal complement \\mathcal{M}^{\\perp}\\coloneqq\\{g:\\langle f,g\\rangle=0\\text{ for all }f\\in\\mathcal{M}\\} . Its orthogonal projection is the unique bounded operator \\mathsf{P}_{\\mathcal{M}} for which \\mathsf{P}_{\\mathcal{M}}f\\in\\mathcal{M} and f-\\mathsf{P}_{\\mathcal{M}}f\\in\\mathcal{M}^{\\perp} . All operators in the convergence analysis are bounded linear operators on the indicated Hilbert spaces unless explicitly stated otherwise. The adjoint \\mathsf{L}^{*} is characterized by \\langle\\mathsf{L}f,g\\rangle=\\langle f,\\mathsf{L}^{*}g\\rangle . An operator is self-adjoint if \\mathsf{L}=\\mathsf{L}^{*} , a contraction if \\|\\mathsf{L}f\\|\\leq\\|f\\| , and unitary if it is onto and preserves norms. A self-adjoint involution, meaning \\mathsf{L}^{2}=I , is unitary. For self-adjoint operators \\mathsf{L}_{1},\\mathsf{L}_{2} , \\mathsf{L}_{1}\\preceq\\mathsf{L}_{2}\\hskip 10.00002pt\\Longleftrightarrow\\hskip 10.00002pt\\langle f,(\\mathsf{L}_{2}-\\mathsf{L}_{1})f\\rangle\\geq 0\\hskip 10.00002pt\\text{for every }f (D.3) is the Loewner order. If \\mathsf{L} is self-adjoint, the spectral theorem defines \\psi(\\mathsf{L}) for bounded Borel functions \\psi ; in particular \\mathbf{1}_{E}(\\mathsf{L}) is the orthogonal projection onto the spectral set E , and a non-negative operator has a unique non-negative square root. Definition D.2 (Markov operators and density evolution) . Let \\mathcal{K}(z,\\mathrm{d}z^{\\prime}) be a Markov kernel preserving \\zeta . Its Markov operator on observables is (\\mathsf{K}f)(z)\\coloneqq\\int f(z^{\\prime})\\,\\mathcal{K}(z,\\mathrm{d}z^{\\prime})\\,. (D.4) It is a contraction on L^{2}(\\zeta) . Its adjoint \\mathsf{K}^{*} governs density evolution: if a law has density h with respect to \\zeta , then its density after one step is \\mathsf{K}^{*}h . Constants are fixed by both operators. If P\\ll\\zeta has density h , then \\chi^{2}(P\\|\\zeta)=\\|h-1\\|_{\\zeta}^{2}\\,. (D.5) Consequently, an operator norm bound for \\mathsf{K} on L_{0}^{2}(\\zeta) gives the same bound for its adjoint \\mathsf{K}^{*} , and hence gives \\chi^{2} contraction."
      },
      {
        "id": "A3.E4",
        "coverage": "EXCLUDED",
        "reason": "Local variance/gradient Dirichlet inequality not new uniqueness dependency.",
        "qualified_raw_slice": {
          "path": "E:/Samplinglib/runs/20261007-companion-priority/pbps-real-defect-root61/next-macro-source62/primary-A3-E4-dffe0a792ef0.raw.snapshot",
